title: Platform
kind: overview
aliases: platforms portability windows macos
summary: Linux is the platform metafolder runs on; the data model is portable, a port to Windows then macOS is under way, and what each Linux-only part does elsewhere.

The ''data'' is platform-independent: paths are stored relative to the repository
root, `/`-separated, as the exact bytes of their names ([[Path representation]]), so a
repository created on one machine can be loaded on another — a repository on a
removable drive, say ([[Moving a repository]]).

The ''programs'' are another matter. A port to Windows, then macOS, is under way
(`roadmap.org`); what runs where, today:

|!Piece |!Linux |!macOS |!Windows |
|daemon, `mf` |built and tested |not built |compiles, tests included (the `windows` check of [[Static checks]]), never run |
|watcher |the inotify source, or the fanotify broker ([[Watch sources and regimes]]) |the `notify` source would place one registration per directory |same |
|`metafolder-watchd` |yes |no (fanotify is Linux-only) |no: the crate compiles empty, the binary says so and exits |
|GUI |yes |refuses to start: it needs `bwrap` ([[Sandboxing the media decoders]]) |compiles, and refuses to start for the same reason |

What the daemon does without Linux, where it compiles:

* ''no broker'': the notify source only, and `GET /watch` says why (`backend_reason`); `[settings] watchd-socket` is not read;
* ''no mount table'': `/proc/self/mountinfo` is Linux's, so mount points are told by the device id ([[Mount point detection]]) and a drive plugged in under a root is seen at the next placement, not as it comes ([[Offline subtrees]]);
* ''no root identity'' off Unix: a root moved away is not noticed ([[When the root moves]]);
* ''no copy in memory'' off Unix: holding a repository in memory needs a tmpfs, and is refused ([[Holding a repository in memory]]);
* ''no free-space probe'' off Unix: the store's map grows by its default room;
* the calibration does not drop the store from the page cache off Linux, and so reads warm.

The tests that exercise what only Unix has — a mode, a symlink, a name that is not UTF-8, the simulated broker, real mounts — are Linux's or Unix's (`cfg`); off Linux the watcher tests run on the notify source only. The bench is a Linux tool, not built for Windows.

And the GUI, off Unix: ''no workspace shell'' — its persistent `bash` runs over pipes, FIFOs and process groups, so a typed line is refused, naming why ([[shell panel]]); a path the shell gives back, and one quoted for it, are taken at their text.

Where the code does branch per platform, both branches are kept honest — the stat
fields that exist only on Unix ([[File metadata fields]]), the token directory
([[Session tokens]]), mount point detection without `/proc` ([[Mount point detection]])
— but nothing exercises the non-Linux ones. A change should not make the port harder
than it is, and should not claim it is done.

The GUI renders in the system WebView (WebKit2GTK on Linux); which audio and video
codecs it can play depends on the GStreamer plugins installed.

